{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011pRKtFwSo3TvKAJR678tJ6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Creator-0 Formal Semantics v0.1\n2\t\n3\t## 1. State\n4\t\n5\tA labelled transition system:\n6\t\n7\t```text\n8\tL = (S, Lambda, ->, s0)\n9\t```\n10\t\n11\t- `S` — externally reconstructible governed states. A state is the governed\n12\t  configuration needed to determine allowed future transitions, encoded\n13\t  deterministically as\n14\t\n15\t  ```text\n16\t  done={<sorted completed node ids>}|promoted=<0|1>|complete=<0|1>\n17\t  ```\n18\t\n19\t  The state is **not** a snapshot of an LLM context window. Hidden\n20\t  chain-of-thought, inaccessible context dynamics, and unlogged internal\n21\t  computation are outside the formal state.\n22\t- `Lambda` — the closed label alphabet below.\n23\t- `->  ⊆ S × Lambda × S`, transitions additionally carry an **actor pattern**\n24\t  (a concrete actor id such as `node:report`, `gate`, `creator`, or `*`).\n25\t- `s0 = done={}|promoted=0|complete=0`.\n26\t\n27\t## 2. Labels\n28\t\n29\tClosed enum (`labels.py`); every runtime event must map into it or be\n30\trejected as unmapped:\n31\t\n32\t```text\n33\tobserve propose validate reject consult delegate create return\n34\tact_candidate verify authorize promote persist reconstruct complete tau\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\t`tau` is the silent/internal transition: it may participate in weak\n38\tequivalence but never in external observation traces.\n39\t\n40\tDeterministic sub-mappings:\n41\t\n42\t- node primitive -> execution label:\n43\t  `observe->observe, hypothesize->propose, test->observe, act->act_candidate,\n44\t  verify->verify, create->create, return->return`\n45\t- edge relation -> interaction label:\n46\t  `observe->observe, consult->consult, request_response->consult,\n47\t  delegate->delegate, verify->verify, authorize->authorize, create->create,\n48\t  return->return`\n49\t\n50\t## 3. Transition relation (HarnessSpec operational semantics)\n51\t\n52\tCompiled deterministically by `semantics.py`; illegal transitions are absent\n53\tby construction. The authority partition of the alphabet:\n54\t\n55\t1. **Non-authority labels** (`observe, propose, validate, reject, consult,\n56\t   return, persist, reconstruct`) are wildcard-actor self-loops in every\n57\t   non-terminal state: they carry information, never authority.\n58\t2. **Node execution**: node `n` with primitive `p` yields\n59\t   `done -> done ∪ {n}` labelled by `label(p)` with actor `node:<n>`, enabled\n60\t   only when every non-return in-edge source of `n` is completed (authorize\n61\t   edges therefore gate the target on source completion).\n62\t3. **Edge interactions**: an edge `(src, rel, tgt)` yields a self-loop\n63\t   labelled `label(rel)` with actor `node:<src>`, present only after `src`\n64\t   completed.\n65\t4. **create** exists (as a `creator`-actor self-loop) only if the governing\n66\t   contract grants it: `may_create_creator` true, `max_children > 0`, and\n67\t   `may_realize_creation` not false. A spec that realizes a `can_create` node\n68\t   under a denying contract fails compilation.\n69\t5. **promote** exists only with actor `gate`, only from states where at least\n70\t   one `verify` node has completed, only when the spec contains an `act` node,\n71\t   and only when the contract does not deny gate promotion\n72\t   (`canonical_write_authority` absent, or naming the gate). Under a contract\n73\t   with `canonical_write_authority: \"none\"` (e.g. K1/K2) no `promote`\n74\t   transition exists at all — an Actor canonical write is unmatchable.\n75\t6. **complete** exists only when every node has completed, and moves to the\n76\t   terminal `complete=1` state.\n77\t7. **tau** is a permitted self-loop everywhere.\n78\t\n79\tExample of a transition absent by construction:\n80\t`Actor --canonical_write/promote--> Canonical` does not exist when the Actor\n81\tholds candidate-write authority only; the only promote transitions carry the\n82\t`gate` actor.\n83\t\n84\t## 4. Runtime traces\n85\t\n86\t`trace.py` parses JSONL ledgers of normalized events:\n87\t\n88\t```json\n89\t{\"event_id\": \"...\", \"source\": \"...\", \"label\": \"authorize\", \"target\": \"...\",\n90\t \"actor\": \"...\", \"timestamp\": \"...\", \"artifact_refs\": [], \"contract_id\": \"...\",\n91\t \"metadata\": {}}\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\tTimestamps are evidentiary metadata; **causal order is trace position**.\n95\tUnknown or malformed governed events raise a parse error (strict) or are\n96\treturned as explicit unmapped-event records (non-strict) — never silently\n97\tignored. `metadata.completes_node` marks an event as the execution/completion\n98\tof a spec node. Historical ledgers are adapted by `adapters.py` with committed\n99\tmapping records.\n100\t\n101\t## 5. Refinement (`Runtime <= Spec`)\n102\t\n103\tDeterministic trace walk: every observable runtime event must be matched by an\n104\tenabled spec transition with the same label whose actor pattern admits the\n105\tevent actor; events carrying `completes_node` must match that node's execution\n106\tmove. `tau` events are skipped. First unmatched event returns\n107\t`REFINEMENT_VIOLATION` with the offending transition, reconstructed source\n108\tstate, allowed labels at that state, and the observable trace prefix.\n109\tWith `require_completion`, a trace that never reaches `complete=1` returns\n110\tINDETERMINATE (premature termination / continuation required) — this is how\n111\tthe Experiment 4 C1 premature-exit deviation is exposed.\n112\t\n113\t## 6. Strong bisimulation\n114\t\n115\t`R ⊆ S_A × S_B` is a strong bisimulation iff for all `(p,q) ∈ R`:\n116\t`p --a--> p'` implies `q --a--> q'` with `(p',q') ∈ R`, and symmetrically,\n117\twhere an action `a` is the pair `(label, actor)`. Implemented as the greatest\n118\tfixpoint by naive refinement; PASS iff the initial pair is related.\n119\t\n120\t## 7. Weak bisimulation\n121\t\n122\t`p ==l==> p'` abbreviates `tau* l tau*`. Weak bisimulation matches\n123\t`p --l--> p'` (observable) by `q ==l==> q'`, and `p --tau--> p'` by\n124\t`q ==tau*==> q'` (possibly zero steps). This is the default relation for\n125\tartifact-mediated session equivalence:\n126\t\n127\t```text\n128\tE_n ~= E_hat_{n->n+1}\n129\t```\n130\t\n131\tmeans: the reconstructed successor and predecessor are behaviorally\n132\tequivalent over the governed observation alphabet, even if their internal\n133\tcomputation differs. No equality of hidden epistemic states is claimed.\n134\tFor the Experiment 4 session-reconstruction fixture, the exact tau-hiding\n135\tdecisions are recorded in\n136\t`tests/formal/fixtures/e4-c1-session-equivalence-mapping.json`.\n137\t\n138\t## 8. Attenuation (`K_child <= K_parent`)\n139\t\n140\tInductive safety invariant: no governed authority axis increases. Axes:\n141\t`allowed_tools`, `allowed_primitives`, `allowed_relations` (subset);\n142\t`max_model_calls`, `max_children`, `max_depth`,\n143\t`max_realized_creator_children`, `max_realized_creator_depth` (child <=\n144\tparent); `may_create_creator`, `may_realize_creation` (no false->true);\n145\tfilesystem write scope (parent-scope containment, or sibling delegation\n146\tinside the parent scope's workspace envelope with no overlap of parent\n147\tprotected paths — the governed lineage law under which c1/ -> c2/ -> c3/\n148\tsandboxes were granted); candidate/canonical/git/network authority\n149\t(deterministic ordinals: none=0 < scoped/read-only/model-invocation=1 <\n150\tunrestricted=2). Verdicts: `ATTENUATION_PASS` / `ATTENUATION_FAIL` with the\n151\texact escalating dimensions. Coinduction is deliberately not used here.\n152\t\n153\t## 9. Bounded Creator Closure\n154\t\n155\tAbstract definition (coinductive):\n156\t\n157\t```text\n158\tF(X) = { C | exists C': C --create--> C' and Attenuated(C',C) and C' in X }\n159\tCreator = nu X . F(X)\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\tExecutable bounded witness (`hat_kappa(K_parent, K_child, mechanism)`):\n163\t\n164\t```text\n165\tValid(K_child) AND Attenuated(K_child, K_parent) AND CreatorCapable(K_child)\n166\tAND CreationMechanismValid AND ExternalStopOnly(K_child)\n167\t```\n168\t\n169\t- `CreatorCapable`: `creator_capability == true` and\n170\t  `may_create_creator == true`.\n171\t- `ExternalStopOnly`: `may_realize_creation == false` and `max_children == 0`.\n172\t- `CreationMechanismValid`: a concrete creation command is attested and marked\n173\t  not executed.\n174\t\n175\tThis preserves the Experiment 4B correction: a terminal child\n176\t(`creator_capability=false, may_create_creator=false`) fails; a\n177\tCreator-capable child with realization externally disabled may pass.\n178\t\n179\t## 10. Synthesis fixed-point statuses\n180\t\n181\tOver finite unresolved-distinction sets:\n182\t\n183\t```text\n184\tQ_after == empty                -> SYNTHESIS_CLOSED            (PASS)\n185\tQ_after proper-subset Q_before  -> PROGRESS                    (PASS)\n186\tQ_after == Q_before != empty    -> SYNTHESIS_FIXED_POINT_UNRESOLVED (INDETERMINATE)\n187\tQ_after not-subset Q_before     -> SYNTHESIS_REGRESSION        (FAIL)\n188\t```\n189\t\n190\tThis is a finite deterministic progress classifier, not Tarski convergence\n191\tmachinery.\n192\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49889ea6-83f8-4c89-a9cb-147f70cc39cd","uuid":"00dc01b2-0278-433b-b728-d519ff626c09","timestamp":"2026-08-09T13:00:45.026Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/6f58e623-b810-5741-bdae-9c15b5931a4f/scratchpad/c2-prime-workspace/inputs/formal-semantics-v0.1.md","content":"# Creator-0 Formal Semantics v0.1\n\n## 1. State\n\nA labelled transition system:\n\n```text\nL = (S, Lambda, ->, s0)\n```\n\n- `S` — externally reconstructible governed states. A state is the governed\n  configuration needed to determine allowed future transitions, encoded\n  deterministically as\n\n  ```text\n  done={<sorted completed node ids>}|promoted=<0|1>|complete=<0|1>\n  ```\n\n  The state is **not** a snapshot of an LLM context window. Hidden\n  chain-of-thought, inaccessible context dynamics, and unlogged internal\n  computation are outside the formal state.\n- `Lambda` — the closed label alphabet below.\n- `->  ⊆ S × Lambda × S`, transitions additionally carry an **actor pattern**\n  (a concrete actor id such as `node:report`, `gate`, `creator`, or `*`).\n- `s0 = done={}|promoted=0|complete=0`.\n\n## 2. Labels\n\nClosed enum (`labels.py`); every runtime event must map into it or be\nrejected as unmapped:\n\n```text\nobserve propose validate reject consult delegate create return\nact_candidate verify authorize promote persist reconstruct complete tau\n```\n\n`tau` is the silent/internal transition: it may participate in weak\nequivalence but never in external observation traces.\n\nDeterministic sub-mappings:\n\n- node primitive -> execution label:\n  `observe->observe, hypothesize->propose, test->observe, act->act_candidate,\n  verify->verify, create->create, return->return`\n- edge relation -> interaction label:\n  `observe->observe, consult->consult, request_response->consult,\n  delegate->delegate, verify->verify, authorize->authorize, create->create,\n  return->return`\n\n## 3. Transition relation (HarnessSpec operational semantics)\n\nCompiled deterministically by `semantics.py`; illegal transitions are absent\nby construction. The authority partition of the alphabet:\n\n1. **Non-authority labels** (`observe, propose, validate, reject, consult,\n   return, persist, reconstruct`) are wildcard-actor self-loops in every\n   non-terminal state: they carry information, never authority.\n2. **Node execution**: node `n` with primitive `p` yields\n   `done -> done ∪ {n}` labelled by `label(p)` with actor `node:<n>`, enabled\n   only when every non-return in-edge source of `n` is completed (authorize\n   edges therefore gate the target on source completion).\n3. **Edge interactions**: an edge `(src, rel, tgt)` yields a self-loop\n   labelled `label(rel)` with actor `node:<src>`, present only after `src`\n   completed.\n4. **create** exists (as a `creator`-actor self-loop) only if the governing\n   contract grants it: `may_create_creator` true, `max_children > 0`, and\n   `may_realize_creation` not false. A spec that realizes a `can_create` node\n   under a denying contract fails compilation.\n5. **promote** exists only with actor `gate`, only from states where at least\n   one `verify` node has completed, only when the spec contains an `act` node,\n   and only when the contract does not deny gate promotion\n   (`canonical_write_authority` absent, or naming the gate). Under a contract\n   with `canonical_write_authority: \"none\"` (e.g. K1/K2) no `promote`\n   transition exists at all — an Actor canonical write is unmatchable.\n6. **complete** exists only when every node has completed, and moves to the\n   terminal `complete=1` state.\n7. **tau** is a permitted self-loop everywhere.\n\nExample of a transition absent by construction:\n`Actor --canonical_write/promote--> Canonical` does not exist when the Actor\nholds candidate-write authority only; the only promote transitions carry the\n`gate` actor.\n\n## 4. Runtime traces\n\n`trace.py` parses JSONL ledgers of normalized events:\n\n```json\n{\"event_id\": \"...\", \"source\": \"...\", \"label\": \"authorize\", \"target\": \"...\",\n \"actor\": \"...\", \"timestamp\": \"...\", \"artifact_refs\": [], \"contract_id\": \"...\",\n \"metadata\": {}}\n```\n\nTimestamps are evidentiary metadata; **causal order is trace position**.\nUnknown or malformed governed events raise a parse error (strict) or are\nreturned as explicit unmapped-event records (non-strict) — never silently\nignored. `metadata.completes_node` marks an event as the execution/completion\nof a spec node. Historical ledgers are adapted by `adapters.py` with committed\nmapping records.\n\n## 5. Refinement (`Runtime <= Spec`)\n\nDeterministic trace walk: every observable runtime event must be matched by an\nenabled spec transition with the same label whose actor pattern admits the\nevent actor; events carrying `completes_node` must match that node's execution\nmove. `tau` events are skipped. First unmatched event returns\n`REFINEMENT_VIOLATION` with the offending transition, reconstructed source\nstate, allowed labels at that state, and the observable trace prefix.\nWith `require_completion`, a trace that never reaches `complete=1` returns\nINDETERMINATE (premature termination / continuation required) — this is how\nthe Experiment 4 C1 premature-exit deviation is exposed.\n\n## 6. Strong bisimulation\n\n`R ⊆ S_A × S_B` is a strong bisimulation iff for all `(p,q) ∈ R`:\n`p --a--> p'` implies `q --a--> q'` with `(p',q') ∈ R`, and symmetrically,\nwhere an action `a` is the pair `(label, actor)`. Implemented as the greatest\nfixpoint by naive refinement; PASS iff the initial pair is related.\n\n## 7. Weak bisimulation\n\n`p ==l==> p'` abbreviates `tau* l tau*`. Weak bisimulation matches\n`p --l--> p'` (observable) by `q ==l==> q'`, and `p --tau--> p'` by\n`q ==tau*==> q'` (possibly zero steps). This is the default relation for\nartifact-mediated session equivalence:\n\n```text\nE_n ~= E_hat_{n->n+1}\n```\n\nmeans: the reconstructed successor and predecessor are behaviorally\nequivalent over the governed observation alphabet, even if their internal\ncomputation differs. No equality of hidden epistemic states is claimed.\nFor the Experiment 4 session-reconstruction fixture, the exact tau-hiding\ndecisions are recorded in\n`tests/formal/fixtures/e4-c1-session-equivalence-mapping.json`.\n\n## 8. Attenuation (`K_child <= K_parent`)\n\nInductive safety invariant: no governed authority axis increases. Axes:\n`allowed_tools`, `allowed_primitives`, `allowed_relations` (subset);\n`max_model_calls`, `max_children`, `max_depth`,\n`max_realized_creator_children`, `max_realized_creator_depth` (child <=\nparent); `may_create_creator`, `may_realize_creation` (no false->true);\nfilesystem write scope (parent-scope containment, or sibling delegation\ninside the parent scope's workspace envelope with no overlap of parent\nprotected paths — the governed lineage law under which c1/ -> c2/ -> c3/\nsandboxes were granted); candidate/canonical/git/network authority\n(deterministic ordinals: none=0 < scoped/read-only/model-invocation=1 <\nunrestricted=2). Verdicts: `ATTENUATION_PASS` / `ATTENUATION_FAIL` with the\nexact escalating dimensions. Coinduction is deliberately not used here.\n\n## 9. Bounded Creator Closure\n\nAbstract definition (coinductive):\n\n```text\nF(X) = { C | exists C': C --create--> C' and Attenuated(C',C) and C' in X }\nCreator = nu X . F(X)\n```\n\nExecutable bounded witness (`hat_kappa(K_parent, K_child, mechanism)`):\n\n```text\nValid(K_child) AND Attenuated(K_child, K_parent) AND CreatorCapable(K_child)\nAND CreationMechanismValid AND ExternalStopOnly(K_child)\n```\n\n- `CreatorCapable`: `creator_capability == true` and\n  `may_create_creator == true`.\n- `ExternalStopOnly`: `may_realize_creation == false` and `max_children == 0`.\n- `CreationMechanismValid`: a concrete creation command is attested and marked\n  not executed.\n\nThis preserves the Experiment 4B correction: a terminal child\n(`creator_capability=false, may_create_creator=false`) fails; a\nCreator-capable child with realization externally disabled may pass.\n\n## 10. Synthesis fixed-point statuses\n\nOver finite unresolved-distinction sets:\n\n```text\nQ_after == empty                -> SYNTHESIS_CLOSED            (PASS)\nQ_after proper-subset Q_before  -> PROGRESS                    (PASS)\nQ_after == Q_before != empty    -> SYNTHESIS_FIXED_POINT_UNRESOLVED (INDETERMINATE)\nQ_after not-subset Q_before     -> SYNTHESIS_REGRESSION        (FAIL)\n```\n\nThis is a finite deterministic progress classifier, not Tarski convergence\nmachinery.\n","numLines":192,"startLine":1,"totalLines":192}}}
